MICRO-INTERACTION 1: THE BOIDS (Ecosystem Physics)

Your specific skills (React, Python, STM32) are not static text—they are
represented by schools of digital marine life.

The Entities (Boids):

Simple, glowing geometric particles or minimalist SVG fish/octopuses.

Text Label: Each boid carries a small, faint text label trailing it
(e.g., React, PyTorch, C++). Typography: DM Sans, 10px.

Flocking Algorithm (The 3 Rules):

Separation: Steer to avoid crowding local flockmates.

Alignment: Steer towards the average heading of local flockmates.

Cohesion: Steer to move toward the average position of local flockmates.

Habitat Binding:

Boids are color-coded to their specific Reef (Category).

They freely swim around, but a gentle gravitational pull keeps them
orbiting their respective home Reefs as the user pans horizontally.

MICRO-INTERACTION 2: FEEDING FRENZY (Cursor Interaction)

The user's cursor actively disrupts the ecosystem.

The Cursor (Bait):

The cursor emits a soft, glowing light radius (#FFFFFF with low opacity).

Hover Attraction: As the cursor moves near a school of Boids, it overrides
their "Habitat Binding". They break off from their reef and gently follow
the cursor.

The Click (Food Drop):

Clicking anywhere in the water drops a "Food" particle (a bright #FCD34D
golden ember) that slowly sinks.

Frenzy Trigger: The max-speed of nearby Boids instantly triples. They dart
aggressively toward the food.

Collision: The first Boid to hit the food consumes it (the golden particle
disappears with a tiny flash), and the flock returns to normal speed.

MICRO-INTERACTION 3: THE SHOCKWAVE (System Reset)

A method to reset the chaos if the user scrambles the ecosystem too much.

Trigger: Clicking the fixed [ DEEP ECOSYSTEM ] header.

The Effect:

A rapid, high-distortion WebGL displacement ring (shockwave) expands outward
from the header text across the entire screen.

Physics Override: All Boids instantly lose their current velocity vectors.

Snapback: They are forcefully pushed back to their exact home Reefs, resuming
their standard, calm orbital pattern.

TECHNICAL UX & PERFORMANCE NOTES

Boids Optimization: Flocking algorithms are mathematically heavy ($O(N^2)$).
Do not exceed 30-50 boids total across the canvas. Use spatial partitioning
(Quadtrees) if performance drops, or limit flockmate checking to a small radius.

Rendering: This entire section MUST be rendered on a single <canvas> element.
Rendering 50 moving DOM elements with text attached will destroy frame rates,
especially during a horizontal scroll. Draw the fillText() for the skills
directly onto the Canvas context.